I absolutely play better on NAO (my very first character on there is sitting
in the castle at the WoW prepping for descent), I play better when showing
someone how to play, and I encourage better play behavior when watching a
friend play on NAO and advising them. The commonality here is that the lag
induced deliberation, or the conversation induced deliberation both result
in more thoughtful actions, and less hasty deaths.

Something like a floating eye death (which I _still_ suffer on occasion
locally, despite only using shift/num5 to move) just doesn't normally happen
when you're playing deliberately. Not only that, you tend to spend more time
contemplating more unusual dangerous situations, and thinking ahead both
about what to do, and how to get out of a situation before it becomes truly
threatening, both enhancing your survival.

It's when your fingers are moving quickly, or you're mentally justifying
foolish risks that confused genocide or gloveless cockatrice corpses
happen...

I'd imagine there's perhaps a similar principle at work when it comes to
notes (not something I could imagine doing, given the number of characters
that tend to bite it, though perhaps there are indeed more lessons to be
learned in your own notes, even moreso if you share them for discussion).

I've also gotta say that after that first elusive ascension, the game feels
more in control (I dare not use the word easy), and the second two were far
less stressful. I'm now enjoying fooling around with various characters, and
even ones I wouldn't have touched before tend to get much farther on average
than my characters in the past.



